{"step_index":305,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-08-24T04:50:54Z","thinking":"**Prioritizing Tool Specificity**\n\nI'm focusing intently on tool specificity. I've been refining my approach to avoid unnecessary use of bash commands for file manipulation. I'm now committed to using `grep_search` directly whenever possible, and only falling back to `grep` inside bash when absolutely required, to improve efficiency. I believe this will streamline my analysis significantly.\n\n\n**Refining Ticker Resolution**\n\nI'm revisiting the ticker resolution logic. My recent focus is understanding why `CIMB.KL` wasn't correctly resolved to `1023.KL`. It seems my `resolve_ticker` function, designed to handle ticker lookups on Yahoo Finance, didn't recognize the user's manual input correctly. I'm investigating the search URL and how the Yahoo Finance API responds to different queries to refine my ticker resolution process. The aim is to make it robust to these kinds of direct, but potentially incorrect, user inputs.\n\n\n**Implementing Ticker Mapping**\n\nI'm now implementing a dictionary within `resolve_ticker` to map common, and often incorrectly entered, ticker names directly to their correct Bursa Malaysia codes. I've begun populating `COMMON_BURSA_MAP` with common examples like \"CIMB\", \"MAYBANK\", and \"SUNCON\" to ensure more accurate resolutions, even with slightly flawed inputs. My goal is to pre-emptively address these common errors to prevent resolution failures. This should greatly improve robustness.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"python -c \"\nfrom stock_screener import resolve_ticker\nprint(resolve_ticker('CIMB.KL', 'Malaysia Stocks'))\nprint(resolve_ticker('CIMB', 'Malaysia Stocks'))\n\"","Cwd":"C:/Users/wai_j/.gemini/antigravity/brain/c155f291-512d-4565-8630-f27b8bc949d1","IsDaemon":false,"WaitMsBeforeAsync":5000,"toolAction":"Checking resolve_ticker behavior for CIMB","toolSummary":"Test resolve_ticker"}}]}
